VERDICT

Correct: your code correctly computes the running sum in-place with optimal linear time.

REVIEW MY CODE

What you did well:

You used a running accumulator variable runSum to avoid re-summing elements from scratch.

You modified the vector in-place, which avoids allocating extra heap memory.

Your code is compact and easy to read.

Is it correct:

Yes, it passes all test cases on LeetCode.

Logical mistakes and edge cases:

No logic errors.

One style detail: nums.size() returns an unsigned type (size_t), while i is a signed int. Comparing them can trigger a compiler sign-comparison warning.

In-place modification alters the caller's input array. In interviews, make sure to ask whether mutating the original array is allowed before doing it.

Edge cases handled properly: single-element array (loop runs once, returns correctly), all negative numbers, and arrays with zeros.

Time and space complexity:

Time complexity: O(n) because it iterates through the vector of size n exactly once.

Space complexity: O(1) auxiliary space because it updates the input array directly and only uses one integer variable.

Why your approach works:

Every prefix sum is just the previous sum plus the current number. By keeping a rolling total in runSum and writing it back to index i, each index gets the exact sum of all elements up to that point.

ALL APPROACHES, FROM BRUTE FORCE TO MOST OPTIMISED

Approach 1: Brute Force (Nested Loops)

Where it comes from:

Standard naive baseline mentioned in LeetCode community discussions and basic programming exercises.

Idea:

For every index i, start an inner loop from index 0 up to i and add up all those numbers from scratch.

C++ Code:

C++
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> runningSum(vector<int>& nums) {
        int n = nums.size();
        vector<int> result(n);
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            int currentSum = 0;
            for (int j = 0; j <= i; j++) {
                currentSum += nums[j];
            }
            result[i] = currentSum;
        }
        return result;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(n^2) because for each element i, the inner loop does i + 1 additions, leading to 1 + 2 + ... + n steps.

Space Complexity:

O(1) auxiliary space (excluding the returned result array).

What makes the next approach better:

Re-adding numbers from index 0 repeatedly is wasteful. The sum up to index i is simply the sum up to i - 1 plus nums[i], so we can do it in a single pass.

Approach 2: Separate Output Array (Non-Mutating Prefix Sum)

Where it comes from:

Official LeetCode editorial Approach 1.

Idea:

Create a new array of the same size. Set the first item to nums[0]. For every next index i, set result[i] to result[i - 1] + nums[i]. This keeps the original input unchanged.

C++ Code:

C++
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> runningSum(vector<int>& nums) {
        int n = nums.size();
        vector<int> result(n);
        result[0] = nums[0];
        for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
            result[i] = result[i - 1] + nums[i];
        }
        return result;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(n) because it visits each element from index 1 to n - 1 once.

Space Complexity:

O(1) auxiliary space if not counting the output array, but O(n) memory allocated to avoid mutating the input.

What makes the next approach better:

If modifying the input vector is allowed, we can eliminate the new array allocation completely.

Approach 3: In-Place Running Sum Using Previous Element

Where it comes from:

Official LeetCode editorial Approach 2 and top-voted solutions.

Idea:

Overwrite nums directly without an extra accumulator variable. Start at index 1 and do nums[i] += nums[i - 1].

C++ Code:

C++
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> runningSum(vector<int>& nums) {
        for (int i = 1; i < nums.size(); i++) {
            nums[i] += nums[i - 1];
        }
        return nums;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(n) because it runs n - 1 additions.

Space Complexity:

O(1) auxiliary space since no extra variables or arrays are allocated.

What makes the next approach better:

In C++, the Standard Template Library already provides a built-in function std::partial_sum that implements this idiom directly.

Approach 4: Standard Library std::partial_sum

Where it comes from:

Modern C++ idiomatic solutions using the header numeric.

Idea:

Use std::partial_sum to calculate running sums across an iterator range. It can write back into the same container in one line.

C++ Code:

C++
#include <vector>
#include <numeric>

class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> runningSum(vector<int>& nums) {
        std::partial_sum(nums.begin(), nums.end(), nums.begin());
        return nums;
    }
};
Time Complexity:

O(n) linear scans performing n - 1 additions internally.

Space Complexity:

O(1) auxiliary space because it updates the vector directly in place.

WHERE I STAND

Which approach you used:

You used a variation of Approach 3: an in-place O(n) time and O(1) auxiliary space solution using an extra rolling variable runSum.

Which approach does an interviewer expect:

Interviewers usually expect Approach 2 or Approach 3. They will often ask: "Are you allowed to mutate the input?" If yes, they want Approach 3 (or your solution). If no, they want Approach 2.

Common follow-up questions and how to answer them:

Question 1: What are the trade-offs of in-place mutation?

Answer: In-place saves memory (O(1) extra space), but mutating the caller's data can lead to bugs in concurrent environments or if another function needs the original array later.

Question 2: What happens if numbers are very large and can cause 32-bit integer overflow?

Answer: If the running sum exceeds 2^31 - 1, standard 32-bit signed ints overflow. The solution would need a return type of vector or 64-bit integer types.

Question 3: How does this prefix sum help solve range sum queries like finding the sum between index L and R?

Answer: Once prefix sums are computed, any range sum from L to R can be answered in O(1) time using runningSum[R] - runningSum[L - 1] (or just runningSum[R] if L == 0).

WHAT I SHOULD REMEMBER

The key insight:

Every prefix sum is simply the previous prefix sum plus the current element.

Checklist for next time:

Check if mutating input is allowed before modifying nums in place.

When doing in-place prefix sums, starting the loop at index 1 and adding nums[i - 1] directly eliminates the need for an extra tracker variable.

Be mindful of sign comparison warnings when comparing int i against nums.size().

The pattern:

Prefix Sum / Cumulative Sum.

